# What must not reach a layer.
#
# The first two entries are the ones that matter: a credential or a client file baked
# into an image is in every copy of that image forever, and an image is a thing people
# push to registries.

.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# client material and run artifacts
inbox/
outbox/
runs/
artifacts/
vault/
**/*.db
**/*.sqlite3
templates/*.xlsx
foundations/cleared.yaml
recipes/registered.yaml
skills-intake/

# local machine state
.venv/
.git/
.github/
# A pattern here is anchored to the context root unless it starts with `**/`, and the
# six below are meant to match at any depth. They did not, and the one that cost
# something was `__pycache__/`: it excluded a directory at the top of the repo that
# does not exist, so 840 `__pycache__` directories and 7,509 `.pyc` files compiled on
# a Windows developer's machine were copied into a Linux image - and Python used them.
# The suite inside the image then ran bytecode rather than the source beside it, which
# is why it reported failures with `C:\dev\automated-analyst\...` in their locations
# and why the image's own job had never passed. An image built from whatever happened
# to be lying around on one machine is the opposite of the claim this file supports.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
docs/.blueprint-mirror

# editor and os
.vscode/
.idea/
**/Thumbs.db
**/.DS_Store

# Interview knowledge can hold client data; it never goes into an image.
knowledge/
